Overlay patch 0054: the VR R9 GAME-SIDE trio — the clipped LAP HUD, the
player's own kart in first person, and the roll/tumble state "Realistic Spins"
needs (VR R9 items 7, 5, 6).

Three edits, three files, all __IOS__-guarded and all gated at runtime on VR
being the thing that is rendering, so the flat game, the 3D panel and the macOS
oracle are byte-for-byte what they were.

  1. src/render_objects.c — `render_texture_rectangle_wide`, THE LAP CLIP.

     the user, 1.0.0.5: the right edge of the LAP element is cut ("LAP 2/" with
     the trailing total gone and the P half-eaten) while TIME renders whole.
     Both elements come out of the SAME helper stack and the same
     scissor/viewport/matrix scope (`func_80058F78` calls `render_hud_timer`
     then `draw_simplified_lap_count`), so nothing about the HUD plane or the
     ortho sentinels is involved. The difference is one branch:

         if ((xl - (width / 2)) < (SCREEN_WIDTH / 2)) {
             coordX  = OTRGetDimensionFromLeftEdge(xl) << 2;
             coordX2 = (xh) << 2;                  // <-- NOT transformed
         } else {
             coordX  = OTRGetDimensionFromRightEdge(xl) << 2;
             coordX2 = OTRGetDimensionFromRightEdge(xh) << 2;
         }

     LAP sits at x≈103 and takes the LEFT branch; TIME sits at x≈228 and takes
     the RIGHT branch, which transforms BOTH edges and is therefore
     width-preserving. The left branch moves the left edge by
     `K = 120*AR - 160` and leaves the right edge alone, so the rectangle's
     width changes by K while the texel rate (`dsdx = 4 << 10`) does not.

     In the flat game K is POSITIVE (any aspect wider than 4:3) and the
     rectangle comes out too WIDE — the texture is drawn shifted but whole,
     which is why this has never been reported. A VR eye is 5087x4081 on
     device: aspect 1.246, NARROWER than 4:3, so K = -10.4 and the rectangle
     comes out ten pixels too NARROW. Ten pixels off a 32-pixel HUD tile is
     precisely the missing "3" and the half-eaten P.

     The fix transforms both edges with the same function, which is what the
     right branch has always done, and it applies ONLY while a VR world frame
     is being drawn (`gSohVRWorldActive`) — the flat/panel HUD keeps its
     existing behaviour to the pixel.

  2. src/render_player.c — `render_kart`, THE OWN KART IN FIRST PERSON.

     the user: his own kart/driver billboard flashes into view, and a 2D
     cardboard cut-out of yourself hanging in the cockpit is distracting.
     Suppressed for the LOCAL player only (playerId == PLAYER_ONE, screenId 0),
     only while `gSohVRHideOwnKart` is set — which the shell raises on world
     frames in FIRST PERSON and nowhere else. Every other kart, the ghost, the
     shadow, the particles and every other mode are untouched.

     REV4 (R16-C step 5): the skip now has an ELSE. When the rear-view pane
     is up the quad is emitted anyway, bracketed by G_NOOP 0x66/0x67, and
     0044 rev12 diverts it into the mirror SINK during the eye passes. A
     mirror that cannot show the machine it is bolted to gives the viewer
     nothing to judge distance against, and this is also falsifier F2: the
     kart's ROW in the pane is 46% of pane height on the 1.0.0.12 look-at
     pivot and 65% on the correct one, measured off the framebuffer.

     The skip is at the GEOMETRY, not at `try_rendering_player`: the kart
     texture arrives by DMA and `render_player`/`load_kart_texture_and_render_
     kart_particles` do blocking `osRecvMesg(&gDmaMesgQueue, ...)` pairs. Skip
     the draw, never the queue.

  3. src/player_controller.c — `func_8002934C`, THE TUMBLE STATE.

     "Realistic Spins (intense)" wants the first-person view to follow the kart
     through a spin-out AND through the airborne hit-somersault. 0051 already
     exports `gSohVRKartSpin` and `gSohVRKartAirborne`; neither carries an
     ANGLE, and the somersault's angle is not in `player->rotation` at all
     (`rotation[0]` and `rotation[2]` are only ever zeroed for a Player).

     What the somersault actually is:
       * `player->unk_0A8` — the tumble phase, 8.8 fixed, one full revolution
         over 0..0x2000; it is what indexes `gKartTextureTumbles`.
       * `player->unk_050[screenId]` — the composed ROLL/lean the kart
         billboard is drawn with, which during HIT_EFFECT is driven by
         `player->unk_D9C`, the real tumble angle.
       * `player->effects & HIT_EFFECT` — "hit an object", the squish/tumble
         case (deliberately NOT part of gSohVRKartSpin since 0051 rev4, whose
         header explains why holding the horizon for a wall scrape is wrong).

     Exported as `gSohVRKartRollBam`, `gSohVRKartTumble` and
     `gSohVRKartHitEffect`. Read by the shell ONLY when the Realistic Spins
     toggle is on; with it off (the default) the composed matrix is bit-for-bit
     what R8 shipped.

  4. src/render_player.c — REV5 (R17-A item 2a), THE OTHER TWO EMITTERS OF THE
     SAME QUAD.

     the user, 1.0.0.13: "when i use a mushroom, i can see faint images and in
     flashes, of my own character's head shaking." Edit 2 above suppresses the
     local kart's quad in `render_kart` and NOWHERE ELSE, and `render_kart` is
     not the only function that draws `gPlayerVtx[playerId]`:

       * `func_80025DE8` (render_player.c) draws the SAME eight vertices again
         as a translucent tinted copy (ZMODE_XLU, `func_8004B614` colour
         effect) under `FrameInterpolation_RecordOpenChild("player_boost", ...)`
         whenever `player->boostPower >= 2.0f`. That is the boost flash, it is
         drawn only while boosting, it carries MK64's boost shake — and it was
         completely unguarded. "Faint images, in flashes, of my own character's
         head shaking" is that function, one clause at a time.
       * `render_player_ice_reflection` draws them a third time, mirrored, on
         ICE surfaces (Sherbet Land). With the kart itself hidden, its
         reflection is a driver with nobody above it.
       * `render_ghost` is the TIME TRIAL ghost and is deliberately left alone:
         it is not you, and the game already draws it for a different player
         record.

     Both get the identical predicate to edit 2
     (`gSohVRHideOwnKart && playerId == PLAYER_ONE && screenId == 0`) around
     the GEOMETRY ONLY — the matrix, the display list, the LUT, the colour
     effect, the render mode and the texture load all still execute, so RSP
     state and the texture DMA are byte-for-byte what they were (0054's
     original rule: skip the draw, never the queue). `gSohVROwnBoostSkips` and
     `gSohVROwnReflSkips` count the suppressions so the suite can assert the
     predicate is IN THE PATH rather than merely compiled in.

     NOTE the rear-view pane: edit 2 has an else-branch that re-emits the kart
     for the mirror under sentinels 0x66/0x67. These two do not, so with
     `rearownkart 1` the pane shows the machine but not its boost flash or its
     ice reflection. That is deliberate — `rearownkart` ships OFF (STATUS open
     question, trap D19) and a second pair of bracketed spans buys nothing
     the user has asked for.

  5. src/render_player.c — REV6 (R18-A), THE CLASS PROBE'S BRACKETS.

     `render_player` wraps the shadow, the kart quad (or the time-trial ghost)
     and the boost flash in G_NOOP 0x70/0x71 pairs whose low 16 bits are
     (class << 12) | playerId: 1/6 = own/other SHADOW, 2/5 = own/other KART
     quad, 3 = own BOOST flash (other karts' boost: 15, not probed). 0044 attributes the FIRST modelview load inside a
     bracket to that class and measures it against the eye recovered from the
     composed eye matrix, walk by walk. Emitted ONLY while the dev knob
     `gSohVRClassProbe` is set (`vr set classprobe 1`); every shipped build and
     every flat frame emit nothing, so the list is byte-for-byte unchanged.

Every write is inside the existing `(playerId == 0) && (screenId == 0)` guard
or a new one exactly like it, so a split-screen or CPU kart can never write the
1P VR state (trap D10).


